<ProseLayout
  title="You learned how to build. The business side deserves the same level of skill."
  description="Developer Business Lab creates practical business systems for independent developers who want stronger offers, better pricing, healthier pipelines, better client decisions, and more control over independent work."
  eyebrow="About Developer Business Lab"
>
  <p>Developer Business Lab exists for technically capable developers who want the business around their skills to work as well as the technical work itself.</p>

  <p>You may already know how to build websites, applications, integrations, internal tools, and custom software. Independent work asks you to become competent at another set of decisions too: what to sell, what the work needs to earn, how buyers understand the value, where opportunities come from, which prospects deserve your time, how a deal becomes paid work, and what should happen after delivery.</p>

  <h2>Technical ability should create options</h2>

  <p>For many developers, freelancing is attractive because valuable technical skills should create more earning upside, more control over the schedule, more choice in clients and projects, and less dependence on one employer or platform.</p>

  <p>Those outcomes depend on the commercial side of the business. A technically strong developer can still underprice work, let acquisition disappear during delivery, accept a weak-fit project because the pipeline is thin, send proposals before the opportunity is ready, or finish good work with no plan for what comes next.</p>

  <p>Developer Business Lab focuses on those decisions.</p>

  <h2>Build business capability, not another pile of advice</h2>

  <p>The products are designed around practical commercial problems. A guide teaches the reasoning behind the decision. A workbook turns that reasoning into something you can use: an offer specification, a pricing policy, a pipeline record, a qualification decision, a commercial record, operating rules, or another artifact that belongs in the business.</p>

  <p>The worked examples show how the reasoning moves from uncertainty to a decision. The objective is not to memorize a framework. It is to become more capable of making the business decision yourself.</p>

  <h2>The catalog follows the business</h2>

  <p>Some problems are narrow. You may already know that pricing, acquisition, sales, proposals, agency partnerships, or recurring work is the part that needs attention. The focused products go deep on those areas.</p>

  <p>Some problems are connected. A weak offer can make marketing vague. A thin pipeline can put pressure on pricing. Poor qualification can create proposal waste. Busy delivery can stop acquisition. In those cases, the Launch Kit and the bundles provide broader operating structure.</p>

  <p>The aim across the catalog is the same: help technical skill produce a business that can support meaningful income, better decisions, stronger client relationships, and more say over how you work.</p>

  <h2>Judge the work by the work</h2>

  <p>The product pages show the current scope, examples, workbook records, proof artifacts, and buying distinctions where they belong. You should be able to inspect what a system teaches before deciding whether it matches the problem you need to solve.</p>

